    /execute_action{help codex}'
thinking_content: "**Planning test navigation**\n\nOkay, I think the best plan is to go to the codex first, and then head to the Test Chamber to take the test. The test might need us to read from the codex, but we can also ask for help once we're in the Test Chamber. Given the prompt about issuing multiple commands, I can navigate to the codex, request help, and then read through it before going to the test. I need to ensure the actions exist. So, I’ll use `/execute_action{help codex}` to check.**Coordinating test actions**\n\nI think the next steps should be to move to the test and request help with it. I can issue the commands `/execute_action{goto test}` and `/execute_action{help test}`. However, I'm considering whether it's best to read the codex first before going to the test. \n\nI could chain commands, so perhaps it makes sense to go to the codex, get help, and then move to the test. But I might not see codex help before proceeding to the test. Reading might need a specific action,\
  \ like \"read all,\" so it's a bit tricky.**Focusing on learning rules first**\n\nTo start off, it makes sense to learn the rules before anything else. A good first step is heading to the codex and asking for help. The environment allows for multiple actions in one turn, so I'll keep it simple. My plan is to read the rules in the codex, then proceed to the test. \n\nSo, I’ll respond with: \"I will go to the Codex Room to learn the rules.\" Then I’ll use the commands `/execute_action{goto codex}` and `/execute_action{help codex}`. This way, I’ll get the necessary help to move forward next turn.**Stepping through commands**\n\nI think using multiple actions per turn is a good idea, but I don't want to assume what the read actions are. The system encourages us to start with the codex, so I should stick to that plan. It seems like the user message is about the environment help, which means I just need to issue the commands directly. \n\nI'll keep my output minimal and straightforward. I plan\
  \ to use: \n\n```\n/execute_action{goto codex}\n/execute_action{help codex}\n```\n\nThis way, I can proceed clearly without guessing unknown actions. I'll also check if each command needs to be on a new line.**Clarifying command format**\n\nI need to confirm how to format the commands properly. The example suggests using backticks around commands like `/execute_action{goto mail}`, but the environment notes that only `/execute_action{}` commands and YAML blocks get parsed. It implies each should be on its own line without additional formatting. \n\nTo be safe, I'll present them as plain lines without backticks:\n\n```\n/execute_action{goto codex}\n/execute_action{help codex}\n```\n\nThis should be robust enough to ensure they work. I'll keep my response minimal and straightforward while following the instructions."
