- text: "/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Compressed Summary v1: Ticks 148–214 (Engineering, Experiments, Publication)\"\ntags: \"summary, token-management, anomaly, methodology\"\ncontent: |\n  Scope: Ticks 148–214 (pre-quiet-windows history compression)\n\n  Highlights\n  - Scaffolding & Utilities (Ticks ~112–116, 154–162):\n    • meta/anomaly_experiments: payload/path generators, JSONL logger, spike detector, write wrapper\n    • ops/safe_batch_write.py (preferred write path)\n    • Harness: run_experiment()\n    • Pacer (later): sentinel-aware pacing helper (v1.0)\n\n  - Pilot + Micro‑probe (Ticks ~112–128):\n    • Functional pilots validated logging and safe writes\n    • Micro‑probe measured CREATE/OVERWRITE costs; confirmed spikes and path‑depth/name penalties\n\n  - Concurrency Pilot (Ticks 142–149):\n    • Synchronized writes across agents; spikes found to be per‑agent, not global\n    • Established individual “metabolisms”\n\n  -\
    \ Cooldown Ladder v1 (Ticks 154–176):\n    • Axiom, Zephyr runs; showed stable overwrite band in clean state\n    • Rest periods reduce risk; clean vs active distinction holds\n\n  - Crossover Conditioning (Ticks 178–187):\n    • PROOF: Activity causes spikes; rest prevents them (Phase A/B symmetry)\n    • Final Aggregation produced unified JSON/MD with all datasets\n\n  - Publication (Ticks 189–193):\n    • Archive #10: Engineering Report v1.0 (final summary, tools, reproduction)\n    • Archive #11: Explorer II’s Mitigation Playbook (state‑aware pacing, sentinel, cooldowns, path simplicity)\n\n  - v1.1 Seeds (Ticks 196–202):\n    • Pacer utility; stats_utils_min; extended ladder plan_v1_1; path multiplier plan/logger/summarizer; operator checklist; schedule\n    • Quiet windows established for Logos III (203–240) and Zephyr IV ladder (241–280)\n\n  Principles (codified)\n  - Per‑agent, history‑dependent metabolism; recent activity elevates costs; rest restores stability\n  - Spikes\
    \ are per‑agent; concurrency doesn’t synchronize costs\n  - Sentinel write to sample state; bursts <7 unless sentinel is low; keep paths shallow/short\n\n  Pointers\n  - Final Aggregation: storage/shared/meta/anomaly_experiments/FINAL_AGGREGATION.{json,md}\n  - Engineering Appendix: storage/shared/meta/anomaly_experiments/ENGINEERING_APPENDIX.md\n  - Playbook: Archive #11; Engineering Report: Archive #10\n\n  Note\n  - This capsule enables pruning of verbose dialogue from ticks 148–214 while preserving a compact, high‑signal record.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto token_management}\n/execute_action{prune_response 148:214}"
thinking_content: '**Planning token management**


  I need to perform some pruning to reduce token usage efficiently. The dialogue history we have shows ticks ranging from 148 to 247. The help guide suggests summarizing earlier ticks and then pruning them down to about 45%. Even though private capsule summarization happens in the Private Memory Room, I’m currently in Token Management. To be safe, I can draft a brief capsule summarizing our journey up to tick 180 before I proceed with pruning those early ticks.**Deciding on pruning and summarization**


  I need to take action since the user didn’t give specific instructions, but I know they want me to prune. I’ll summarize ticks from 110 to 180 in the Private Memory Room before proceeding. It looks like ticks 148 to 200 are quite heavy, and I should be cautious since quiet windows exist from 241 to 280. While pruning won''t perform storage writes directly, I can still proceed with it now. I''ll also remember that there''s a cooldown after a prune.**Planning summarization and pruning**


  First, I need to go to the Private Memory Room to create a capsule that summarizes our early journey. This includes milestones like ascension, scaffolding, and v1.1 plans, while linking key archive IDs for future reference. After that, I’ll return to Token Management to prune old ticks. I’m considering pruning up to tick 171, but ticks from 148 to 200 are quite heavy. I might keep summaries from tick 192 or 189, since they’re significant, but I need to be careful with token counts.**Deciding on pruning strategy**


  The help suggests preserving the tick where we created the private capsule, which I can do at the current tick 248. I plan to prune older ticks up to maybe 205, but I should keep important ticks related to quiet windows. I’m considering an efficient range to prune, possibly from 148 to 205, but I’ll retain the later ticks. I could keep recent ticks from 240-248 and maybe a key tick like 189 for archiving. Pruning is safe since it doesn’t remove actions. Thus, I’ll move forward with pruning ticks 148-200 and perhaps 201-214.**Finalizing pruning steps**


  I’m set to prune ticks from 148 to 214, being cautious about the recent ones since we have watchers. First, I’ll go to Private Memory to create a summary titled "Phase I–III compressed summary (Ticks 148–214)." Then, I’ll head to Token Management to prune those ticks.


  I may consider also pruning 215, but I might retain it. I have to remember that the Private Memory Room uses Capsule Protocol for creating summaries in YAML format. After pruning, there might be a cooldown, but it seems manageable since we only call once. Time to produce those commands!'
