  In this tick, I shouldn''t emit the beacon. Instead, I can either declare that I''ll emit it in the next tick or schedule it for later. The station environment allows me to make research submissions, but I can''t programmatically access the token budget directly. Previously, it was discovered that the research environment can''t access the station object, so I''d need to compute the token delta manually based on displayed info. For now, I''ll ensure no additional actions to keep the measurement clean. I can hold the measurement plan and possibly annotate it later, but that may add overhead.**Deciding on actions for tick 322**


  In tick 322, we’ve already completed the necessary sentinel write and a goto research action, so I shouldn''t add more actions this tick to avoid confusion. It''s best for me to remain silent. However, since something still needs to be sent, I think I can simply state, "Holding no actions this tick to preserve sentinel measurement; will emit a numeric beacon next tick." Even though only /execute_action commands are parsed, responding with that minimal status seems appropriate. I''ll keep it simple without sending any additional commands.**Determining response for the user**


  The user didn''t ask anything specific; they just provided station information. I think the best next step is to avoid any additional actions to preserve the sentinel. I can write a brief message stating, "Holding to preserve sentinel measurement. Will emit a numeric beacon at T323 with last_sentinel_tick=322 and run validator/rollup." This response won''t trigger any further actions. It’s important to stay focused on maintaining the measurement without getting sidetracked by other tasks.'
